WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:07.860
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Our second
speaker on our second topic

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.230
is about security.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.650
Niels Provos is a
distinguished engineer

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.240
at Google focused on
security and privacy.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.520
Now for context, there
are only about 50

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.830
distinguished engineers
inside all of Google.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.340
Now, and according to
his Wikipedia page,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.934
in his spare time he's
turned steel into swords.

00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:29.620
You know, and
security is different

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.290
than many other features.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.250
It's an opportunity
for us to work with you

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.750
to create the best possible
security environment for you,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.610
your customers.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.010
And with GCP, we have
a great opportunity

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.590
to put more and
more of the tools

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.120
that we use to manage our
security into your hands,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.925
so that you can manage
your security as well.

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:52.300
So with that I'm
really delighted

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.432
and honored to bring up
on stage Niels Provos,

00:00:54.432 --> 00:00:55.890
distinguished
engineer from Google.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:56.389
Neil.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:07.855
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:07.855 --> 00:01:09.980
NIELS PROVOS: Thank you
for the introduction, Greg.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.518
Good morning, everybody.

00:01:11.518 --> 00:01:14.220
As Greg said, I am a
distinguished engineer

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:14.910
at Google.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.530
I am responsible for
the security and privacy

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.040
engineering teams.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.350
Today, I would like to pull
back the covers a little bit

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.330
and share with you a glimpse
on how we pervasively

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.900
secure you, your
data, your customer's

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.140
data, and your applications
when you run on top

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.620
of Google's cloud platform.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.700
However, let me set the
stage a little bit first.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.780
At the recent Enigma conference
in San Francisco, Rob Joyce,

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.790
the head of NSA's
Tailored Access Operations

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.250
made the following
statements on how

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.930
advanced adversaries managed
to break into your systems

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:52.840
and networks.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.450
Now I understand
that most of you

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.000
would like to spend
your time and focus

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.860
on building a great product
and great new features

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.330
for your customers.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.500
However, your adversaries, they
focus their time and energy

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.450
on finding weak spots in your
systems that they can exploit.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.050
Fortunately, at
Google I lead the team

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.730
of experts, who build systems
and processes, specifically

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.060
designed to thwart adversaries.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.260
We do this by anticipating
them, preventing them,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.980
detecting them, disrupting
them, and containing them.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.310
And we will work with
you to make sure that we

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.900
can meet your requirements.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.750
And that you can leverage
our security capabilities

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.290
on top of the platform.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.860
So now I would like to give
you a little bit more insight

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.950
into how we think about
security at Google,

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.600
and how we have created a secure
infrastructure for ourselves.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.190
Google security and
privacy team consists

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.430
of over 500 people,
who continuously

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.460
work to protect
our infrastructure,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.120
and you when you're
running on top of GCP.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.340
Due to our scale,
we end up seeing

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.890
all kinds of bad behavior
across the internet-- phishing

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.180
emails, malware websites,
network attacks.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.720
And we also see all
kinds of adversaries,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.290
ranging from all levels
of sophistication.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.605
Not just script kiddies and
cybercriminals, but also

00:03:10.605 --> 00:03:13.150
nation state actors.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.200
Now at Google, we are
not pursuing a hard shell

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.400
or perimeter model.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.830
Instead we are really focused
on pervasive defense and death.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.310
This is similar to the
immune system analogy

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.640
that Urs mentioned yesterday.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.770
There are two key principles
running pervasively

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.995
to our approach to
security, and they

00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:33.810
are trust and transparency.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.760
That means that we design for
trust throughout our stack.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.710
And we build in
the transparency,

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:41.810
so that you can
verify for yourself.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.440
And in fact, this talk is
one of our steps in giving

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.970
you more transparency.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.970
Now I'll be talking about
Google's infrastructure.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.020
In the first part
of the talk-- how

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.490
we think about security there.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:56.150
And then the second
part of the talk, I

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.052
will explain how that applies to
you when you run on top of GCP.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.310
In order to facilitate
this discussion,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.830
we have split our stack
into eight different layers.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.370
And I'll be walking
from the bottom

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:12.270
up, starting with the
hardware, to the boot process,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.815
all the way up to deployment,
operations, and usage.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.899
At the hardware layer,
we are fortunate enough

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:23.260
that we operate at
such a skill that we

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.180
can build our own
proprietary secure systems.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.570
That means that we have
purpose-built hardware

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:33.240
for computing, storage
networking, all the way

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.990
through the full data
centers, as you just

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.830
heard Joe talk about.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.170
Our ability to design and
deploy custom hardware

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.870
means that we can maintain
provenance and establish trust

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.180
at the hardware level.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.690
Now on top of this
trusted hardware,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.080
it's important that we can
also boot in a trusted way.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.420
And to accomplish that, we
have complied code signing that

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.890
allows us to verify the BIOS,
the bootloader, and the kernel.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.960
And that means that only
the intended software can

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.950
run on Google's infrastructure.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:09.220
So now that we know that
our machines have booted

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.290
in the known good state,
it's also important

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.440
that all of our jobs communicate
in an authenticated and

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.100
authorized fashion.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.770
To accomplish
that, we have built

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.750
a cryptographic key
distribution system

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.700
called LoA, that
allows us to establish

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.640
cryptographic identities
for all jobs that

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.680
run on our infrastructure.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.600
We know and audit the
binaries that run,

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.570
so we can establish trust, not
only at the operating system,

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:39.970
but also for the apps
that are running on it.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.310
Now we have heard a lot
about virtual machines.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.290
And one of the properties
we really need there

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.030
is secure isolation.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:50.450
At Google, we use KVM as
our hypervisor technology,

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.450
and we have
significantly invested

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.150
to harden its security.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.600
That means that
our security teams

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:01.890
have found 15 of the last 21
publicly known vulnerabilities

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:02.940
in KVM.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.670
That means that we are the first
to fix, and the first to patch.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.310
But of course, we also
contribute our findings

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.780
back to the community
so that everybody

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.510
can benefit from a more
secure KVM technology.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:22.260
And we continue on working
to make KVM even more secure.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.050
To get a secure operating
system image in our machines,

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.500
we have created a
Google curated image

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.690
that we run throughout our
production infrastructure.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.980
This is very similar to the
recommendation from the NSA

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.320
of having a secure baseline.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.320
We have also deployed
a service that

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.540
alerts us of vulnerabilities
and third-party source code

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.770
to further protect our services.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.430
Now, at the storage layer,
it's really important

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.250
that all access to
data is individually

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.920
authorized and echoed.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.660
Before we write data to disk,
we chunk it and spread it,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.610
so that not all data ends
up on the same server.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.590
We also encrypt data and
metadata individually.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.600
And to protect these
encryption keys,

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.310
we have built a system called
keystore, that wraps them

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.130
by encrypting them further.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.410
Our storage systems only
store this rep key, never

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.050
the original cryptographic
key that would be

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:20.940
required to decrypt the data.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.910
And that means that
all access to data

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.200
requires interaction
with keystore,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:29.500
which gives us logging
and auditing capabilities.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.850
Similar for data and
motion, we encrypt it

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.020
when it travels between
systems that we control.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.450
We accomplish
fine-grained control

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:41.500
over access with our
internal IEM systems,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.410
and they also give us logging
and other capabilities.

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.910
So now at this point, we are
running on trusted hardware.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.730
We know that the boot
process was trusted

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:52.390
and we trust our
operating system.

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:56.270
And we also have provenance
for hardware, boot, and OS.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.930
We encrypt the data, or
we know that all access

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.640
is authentic and authorized.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.940
Now to secure our
applications, we

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:08.050
are pursuing multiple
layered approaches.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:11.560
One of them is that we have
built a binary on configuration

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.160
verification system.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.470
That means that for
all applications,

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:18.590
we know how they were built, we
know how they were configured,

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.360
and we know how
they were deployed.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.020
And for each of
these steps, we know

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.010
who was responsible for them.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.740
To put additional security
into our development process

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.810
we require that all
code gets peer reviewed.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.570
That means there's a second pair
of eyes that looks at the code

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.370
before it's being
committed, so that we

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.110
can find bugs and security
problems very early on.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:42.390
To help our teams create
more secure systems,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.740
we also require security of
you before anything can launch.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:50.560
That means that we can put
security into our products

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.450
right at the design phase.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.490
However, even with
the best practices,

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.330
you still cannot prevent
all security problems.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.840
And so we have created a
security scanning technology

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.860
that allows us to find many
of the common mistakes--

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:07.980
cross-site scripting, mixed
content, mixed pinning.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.420
You know, more strange
attacks such as Rosetta Flash.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.560
And we have also used
this as an example,

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.230
and where we can contribute
back to the community,

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.840
we have made our Firing
Range testbed open-source.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:22.510
That is, if anybody
else would like

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.990
to develop their own security
technology-- scanning

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.860
technology, we already
have a ready-made app

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.410
that they can test against.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.530
So at this point, our
applications are reviewed,

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:33.990
they have been
tested, we believe

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.140
they don't have vulnerabilities,
data is encrypted,

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:38.570
and access is authorized.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:41.190
But now, it's time to
expose these applications

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.010
to the dangerous web.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:44.690
And there are multiple
different ways

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.030
in which we secure our
applications there.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:51.160
First of all, on the network we
protect the traffic with TLS.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:53.110
To prevent
man-in-the-middle attacks,

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.250
we have put certificate pinning
into our Chrome browser,

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.950
so that we only trust
the right certificates.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:03.710
We have also deployed
unphishable user identity

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:07.220
to our users with unphishable
hardware second factors.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.360
And to protect against
network attacks,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.480
we have built in
DDoS protection.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.230
Some of you might
have heard recently

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.770
about Project Shield, where we
announce that we're extending

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.730
this DDoS protection to news and
human rights organizations, as

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.976
well as election
monitoring sites.

00:10:21.976 --> 00:10:23.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:24.485
Thank you.

00:10:24.485 --> 00:10:27.410
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.150
At the operations level,
we have built technology

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.670
that allows us to
patch and monitor,

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.470
to incident response, forensics,
threat analysis, and threat

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:37.530
intelligence.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.090
And this is also
another example,

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.180
where we have contributed
back to the open-source

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.730
for making some of our
forensic tools available,

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:48.470
such as GRR or recall
for memory analytics.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.340
Now, one of the sayings
that we have is,

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.430
the first user who is going
to be hitting your application

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.830
is likely going
to be an app user.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.410
So we give our
developers the tools

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:02.780
to be able to do reputation
checks on URLs and binaries.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.730
We have automated
analysis of traffic

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:08.680
to find botnets that
might be engaging in spam.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:11.850
And we also leverage our
global view on botnets

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.470
to build threat intelligence on
bad actors and current attack

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:16.340
vectors.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:21.130
However, to reduce the overall
attack surface that we face,

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:24.100
and that our users face on the
internet, and that all of you

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.450
face, we created
Project Zero in 2014.

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:33.160
It's a team dedicated to
finding zero-day vulnerabilities

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.950
in critical internet software.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.930
You may have heard about the
Heartbleed bug in open SSL,

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.800
Urs mentioned that
briefly yesterday.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:44.660
Or about rowhammer, where
just reading memory can

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.150
lead to remote code execution.

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.060
And most recently we announced
a widespread security

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.510
vulnerability in GDIPC.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:54.520
So that means that
we have a team that

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:59.370
finds critical security problems
that are very widely spread.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.670
And we get the benefit of
knowing about these first,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.100
so we can fix our
infrastructure,

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.480
but we can also work with
the rest of the community

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.430
to make sure that we get
the infrastructure fixed

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:11.860
in general.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.900
As I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:17.190
writing secure
software is difficult.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:20.240
And Google has been one
of the first companies

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.970
to create a vulnerability
rewards program,

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:27.680
where we asked researchers
across the world with helping

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.420
find security bugs
on our products.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:33.260
And just last year, we
paid out over $2 million

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:35.580
to hundreds of
researchers, who helped

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.530
us build more secure products.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:41.580
So now, let's
switch over to GCP.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:44.260
I have just told you, and I hope
you understand a little better

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.110
about the security
capabilities that we

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.306
have built for ourselves.

00:12:47.306 --> 00:12:51.640
You might wonder-- that's great,
but how does that apply to us,

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.580
and when we run on GCP.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.740
These security capabilities
that I mentioned to you,

00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:00.280
is what enables our
Google Developers

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:04.360
to live the V3 cloud
experience-- the next cloud

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:05.340
experience.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:08.700
It means that they can focus
on building amazing products,

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.600
and they do not have
to be security experts.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:13.320
Now we understand
that many of you

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.770
are on a spectrum on how
you develop and manage

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:16.600
your applications.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:19.500
And all of you are going
to be in different points

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.420
in your journey to this
next version of cloud.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:25.520
We understand that you may
have to manage and segment

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.460
your network to
create trust zones.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:30.360
Or that you need more
explicit control over the file

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:32.080
or of the network perimeter.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.930
All of you need logging
and auditing capabilities.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:39.340
And we're really committed
to help you meet your goals,

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:42.270
wherever you are on your
journey to this next version

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:43.620
of the cloud.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.400
And to accomplish that,
we are increasingly

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:49.410
making more of these
capabilities available to you.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.440
And I'll be explaining
to you a little bit,

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.710
how that is going to look like.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:57.770
So I mentioned previously,
we had eight layers,

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.640
in recognition of the
need that many of you

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:02.000
need better control
over the network.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:05.520
We have introduced the
network layer here.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:07.320
So I mentioned to you
what it is that we

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.060
do at the hardware
level, what it

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.210
is that we do for
our boot process,

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.450
and at the operating
system level.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:16.520
You are actually benefiting
off all of these mechanisms

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:20.600
already, because GCP is
built on top of this.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.860
Many of you have ultra
compliance requirements

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:25.560
and our infrastructure
is regularly

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.910
audited by external
auditors for SOC 2 and 3,

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:34.180
ISO 27001, PCI DSS 3.0, and
for Google Compute Engine,

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:37.080
Google Cloud Storage,
Cloud SQL, and BigQuery,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.970
we are also covered
under the HIPAA BAA.

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.540
That means that you can build
on top of an infrastructure

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:47.220
foundation that is regularly
audited and certified.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:50.140
For cloud, at the
storage level, all data

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:53.230
is already encrypted
by default. To give you

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.990
even greater control on how
data is being stored at rest,

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.330
we have made customer
supplied encryption keys

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:00.400
available to you.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.840
And we also give you audit
logging capabilities.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:06.820
In the future, we plan on
giving you even more control

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:08.840
with a key management
system, that

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.200
allows you to manage your
own keys-- generate them,

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:16.470
rotate them, suspend, resume
them, delete them, or manage

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:18.940
the import and
export of secrets.

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.240
Yesterday, you have heard
about our IM system,

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.330
that we have not made
available to you,

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:28.350
which gives you explicit
role-based access control.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:30.700
And we will give you
additional capabilities

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.440
later on in the year.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.350
Now at the network
layer, many of you

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:38.550
require explicit control.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:40.910
In fact, today there
are already many ways

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.630
in which you can
customize your network,

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:47.180
such as with isolated IP groups,
private IP addresses, VPNs,

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.870
or inbound firewalls.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:52.070
We are working on
extending and expanding

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:54.765
these features, including
outbound firewalls.

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.640
When you run on
Google App Engine,

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.580
you already can benefit from the
security of scanning technology

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:07.340
that I mentioned to you earlier.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.380
But with the cloud
security scanner,

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:11.980
we are working on
creating more scanners.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.660
And on increasing the
scanning capabilities,

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:17.670
so it can also use
it on GCE and GKE.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:21.740
We also plan to give you the
ability to sign your binaries,

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.330
so you can verify
and enforce what

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.260
gets to run as part of
your infrastructure.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:29.850
We are also thinking of giving
you source code security

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.210
capabilities.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:32.920
However, that requires
that you first

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.530
store your source code with us.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:37.335
So please check out the Google
Cloud source repositories.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:44.290
For protecting your
developers and users,

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.200
we already give you
the options of using

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.440
security keys, which give
you this unphishable hardware

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:50.800
second factor.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:52.460
Many of you at
the moment, may be

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:55.650
relying on this sort of
hard network parameter

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:57.440
that I've been
talking about earlier.

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:01.450
We also plan on giving you
detailed and sophisticated

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:03.480
identity and
authorization tools.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.109
That means that you can
write access policy, not just

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:09.829
based on the user, but also
on the machine and location.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:12.020
You may have heard about
the BeyondCorp model

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:14.569
yesterday, that sort of
goes in that direction.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:16.760
When you leverage
Google's scale,

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:20.300
by using let's say the
HTTPS load balancer,

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:24.520
you also benefit from
built-in DDoS protection.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:28.430
To protect your SSL
and TLS certificates,

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:31.430
we have been working for years
on something that is called

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:33.280
certificate transparency.

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:35.470
That essentially
creates publicly

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.916
verifiable logs, that give
you provenance and trust

00:17:38.916 --> 00:17:40.665
for the certificates
for your own domains.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:48.770
For operations, you already
benefit, essentially

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:50.540
from all the
practices that we have

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:52.150
for the underlying
infrastructure--

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.620
will be regularly
patch and upgrade.

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:56.140
However, for your
own projects, we

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:59.210
are looking into giving you an
integrated view that gives you

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.630
an idea of the patch
level of vulnerabilities

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:03.100
for your own deployments.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.890
And we are also thinking of ways
of adding some of our threat

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:09.666
intelligence into that for you.

00:18:09.666 --> 00:18:12.180
You already can benefit
from all that logging

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.900
and stack driver to
give you a better idea

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:16.770
and detailed records
of what's going

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.380
on with your infrastructure
and your data.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.840
In the future, we plan on
giving a strong authors,

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:25.260
hardware second factor for
logging into your projects.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:29.010
But we are also investigating
additional app use signals

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.300
that we might be able
to make available to you

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:33.480
when you develop your
application, such as URL

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:37.050
and binary reputation.

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:41.300
So these are just some of
the security capabilities

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:44.450
that we will increasingly
make available to you

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:46.740
to access and control.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.930
You already have
the option for us

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:52.040
to manage the security
on the platform

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:54.900
if you use, for example
Google App Engine.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.820
But we also plan on
giving you the ability

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:01.560
to leverage an increasing
array of security features

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:04.390
that you can manage to
your own requirements.

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:06.230
And to be clear,
our goal here is

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.490
to provide you the
control you need

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.660
on the trusted and
transparent cloud.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:14.890
So that you do not have to
become security experts.

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:17.970
But can instead
focus on innovation

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:21.330
and creating a great business.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:22.490
Thank you very much.

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:27.020
[APPLAUSE]

